  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...A weak secondary cold front may produce a few \nscattered light showers through this morning, then gradual clearing \nthis afternoon.\n\nA weak secondary cold front will move southeast across the area this \nmorning. Convergence along the boundary will combine with modest \nlake enhancement as temperatures cool aloft to support a few \nisolated to widely scattered showers this morning. Coverage of rain \nwill remain sparse, but a few locations may see a passing shower or \ntwo especially across the Genesee Valley, Finger Lakes, and higher \nterrain of the North Country through mid to late morning. \n\nSome patchy fog will continue through early to mid morning, mainly \nacross the Southern Tier, Finger Lakes, and North Country.\n\nThe airmass quickly dries this afternoon as high pressure builds \ninto the eastern Great Lakes. Any scattered showers will end by \nmidday, with clouds giving way to increasing sunshine this afternoon \nfrom northwest to southeast. \n\nKey Message 2...Dry weather will return tonight and last through \nmost of the weekend.\n\nSurface high pressure will settle overhead tonight, with clear skies \nand light winds allowing for strong radiational cooling. Lows will \ndrop into the low to mid 50s on the lake plains, with 40s across the \nSouthern Tier and North Country. A few of the normally cooler \nvalleys of the Southern Tier and Tug Hill region may drop to the \nlower 40s.\n\nThe surface high will drift east off the New England coast Saturday, \nthen linger across the Mid Atlantic through Sunday. Dry weather will \nprevail areawide Saturday through most of Sunday. A frontal zone \nwill begin to develop from the upper Great Lakes to eastern Ontario \nby late in the weekend with thermal packing between a building ridge \nover the central US and a lingering trough across Quebec. This will \nallow a few showers and thunderstorms to develop north of the region \nby Sunday afternoon, and a few of these may reach the North Country \nby late in the day. Otherwise, expect dry weather to continue \nthrough Sunday afternoon for the rest of the area. A better chance \nof a few showers and thunderstorms will arrive by Monday and Tuesday \nas the frontal zone gradually sags south.",
